---
/**
 * About / trust page: review board (empty seats shown on purpose),
 * editorial policy, accessibility commitment, crisis note.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import EmpathyCallout from '../components/EmpathyCallout.astro';
import HandoffCTA from '../components/HandoffCTA.astro';
import InlineDisclaimer from '../components/InlineDisclaimer.astro';

// Hidden per Mike (2026-09-06) until reviewer recruiting is further along —
// flip to true to restore the review-board and editorial-policy sections.
const SHOW_TRUST_SECTIONS = false;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Waypoint — how we earn trust"
  description="Every Waypoint page names its author and reviewer, cites the statute behind every claim, logs changes to every figure, and corrects mistakes in public."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="How Waypoint earns trust"
    title="Verifiable, or it doesn't publish."
    lede="You're making decisions about your child's services based on what you read here. So every page names its author and its reviewer, cites the statute behind every claim, carries a change log on every dollar figure, and corrects its mistakes in public. If a page can't meet that bar, it doesn't go up."
  />

  {SHOW_TRUST_SECTIONS && (
  <div class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">The review board</span>
      <h2>The people behind the pages</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Two of these three seats are still being recruited — and we show them empty on purpose.
        You deserve to know exactly who has, and who hasn't, checked a page yet.
      </p>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Mike Beebe</h3>
          <p>
            <b>Parent-founder · Oakland, CA · Author.</b> Writes the first draft of every guide
            from lived experience navigating Regional Centers, IEPs, and Medi-Cal for his own
            family. Experience is his credential — and the only one he claims. Legal and
            benefits accuracy is the reviewers' job, and pages say so until those seats are
            filled.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>[Reviewer seat — recruiting]</h3>
          <p>
            <b>Special-education attorney.</b> Will review every IEP, IDEA, and Ed Code page
            before it sheds its "pending expert review" label. Until this seat is filled, those
            pages carry the label right at the top — no pretending.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>[Reviewer seat — recruiting]</h3>
          <p>
            <b>Benefits advocate / former service coordinator.</b> Will review Regional Center,
            IHSS, Medi-Cal, and SSI content — the pages where a wrong sentence can cost a family
            real money. Someone who has sat on the agency side of the table, checking our work
            from yours.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section on-white">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Editorial policy</span>
      <h2>How a page gets published</h2>
      <article class="prose">
        <h3 id="ai-disclosure">AI-assisted drafting, disclosed</h3>
        <p>
          Waypoint drafts with AI assistance, and we say so — here and on the pages themselves.
          AI makes us faster. It does not get a byline, and it never gets the last word.
        </p>
        <h3 id="human-review">Two humans before publish</h3>
        <p>
          Every page gets a founder edit, then credentialed review. Nothing that touches your
          money, your child's services, or your legal rights publishes unreviewed. While
          reviewer seats are being recruited, affected pages carry a visible "pending expert
          review" label instead of a borrowed air of authority.
        </p>
        <h3 id="citations">Every claim cites a primary source</h3>
        <p>
          Statute, regulation, or an official agency publication — the way you've seen
          <Cite>WIC §4643</Cite> and <Cite>CA Ed Code §56321</Cite> chips across this site. If
          we can't cite it, we cut it. That standard exists so you can hand any Waypoint page to
          a service coordinator or an attorney and have it hold up.
        </p>
        <h3 id="change-logs">Dollar figures get dates and change logs</h3>
        <p>
          Benefit amounts, income limits, and hour caps change. Any page carrying a dollar
          figure or a rate shows its last-updated date and a visible change log, so you can see
          what changed, and when, before you rely on it.
        </p>
        <h3 id="corrections">Corrections are public</h3>
        <p>
          When we get something wrong, we fix the page, record the correction in its change
          log, and leave the record up. Quiet edits erode trust. Corrections, made where
          everyone can see them, build it.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
  )}

  <div class="section on-cream">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Accessibility</span>
      <h2>Built to be usable by the people it's for</h2>
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          Waypoint's audience includes disabled parents and disabled young people. A site about
          disability rights that fails with a screen reader, a switch, or a magnifier would be a
          contradiction in terms — so this site has to lead by example.
        </p>
        <p>
          We commit to <b>WCAG 2.2 AA</b> and audit against it. Where we fall short, that's a
          bug, and we'd like you to file it like one. Accessibility reports go to the top of
          the queue.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rather than summarize that here — where it would quietly drift out of date — the
          full statement lives in one place: what gets tested before every deploy, what has
          never been tested, and how to report a barrier. Read
          <a href="/accessibility/">our accessibility statement</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section on-white">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">A note on safety</span>
      <h2>Before anything else</h2>
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          If you're in crisis while navigating all of this — or you're not sure, which is reason
          enough — <b>call or text 988</b>, the Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline. It's free, 24/7,
          in English and Spanish. Waypoint is a guide, not an emergency service.
        </p>
      </article>
      <EmpathyCallout label="To the parent reading at 2 a.m.">
        Caregiver burnout is not a character flaw. It's the predictable result of doing a
        caseworker's job, a nurse's job, and a parent's job at once — unpaid, untrained, and
        usually unthanked. Getting help, for your child and for yourself, isn't stepping away
        from the fight. It's how you stay in it. You found this page. You're still going. That
        counts.
      </EmpathyCallout>
      <HandoffCTA
        heading="See the receipts for yourself"
        body="Every answer in the Waypoint app carries the same statute citations you see on this site. Free, instant, no email gate."
        action="Try Waypoint free →"
        slug="about"
        cta="about-footer"
      />
      <InlineDisclaimer variant="legal" />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
